    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/Digital-and-Audio-Account-Executive_R9523-1": {
      "title": "Digital and Audio Account Executive",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-31\nFull time\n\nThe Digital and Audio Account Executive executes and leads the business to business portion of WFIT's fundraising strategy. This included selling WFIT's media assets: web and digital products, on-air business and prospecting and selling new business. Understands the WFIT brand, programming, AAA music and NPR new programming. Publishes web stories and other digital elements about sponsors. Responsibilities Include: Attend external functions (e.g. Chamber of Commerce meetings and other community based events) where potential or existing clients may be in attendance.   Use AI technologies in accordance with station policies to affect sales materials and copy. Work with the General Manager to set forecasts and sales goals for on-air, events and other sellable media assets. Participate in membership fund drives including on-air pitching. Participate as a collaborative member of the WFIT management team and staff in developing and creating an integrated approach in supporting all WFIT fundraising, marketing, programming and outreach efforts.  Delivering compelling, solutions based proposals in professional packages. Produce and edit stories on wfit.org to include sponsor lists and highlights.  Insert sponsor ads into web site. Write and produce stories about sponsors for WFIT’s newsletter. Requirements Include: Bachelor's degree in Administration, Communications or other related field preferred. 2-5 years of relevant experience required. Experience in B2B sales, account development, new business and agency management. Keen understanding of digital media and prior success selling integrated programs that include digital broadcast and events. Ability to apply research in the sales process. Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities. The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following website 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Institute-of-Technology/School-of-Arts-and-Communication-Post-Doc-Visiting-Research-Associate_R9285": {
      "title": "School of Arts and Communication Post Doc Visiting Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-31\nFull time\n\nThe School of Arts and Communication at Florida Institute of Technology seeks a Postdoctoral Research Associate as an active participant in the project “Cultivating Core Character through the General Education Core,” funded by Wake Forest University and the Lilly Endowment Inc. under co-PIs Lars Jones, Andrew Aberdein, and Heidi Hatfield Edwards. Beyond pursuing their own research agenda, the successful candidate will work under the supervision of project PIs and is expected to begin the 12-month annual position in January 2027 and continue through the end of the grant term in July 2029. Funds available for approved grant-related travel. Salary supplements may also be available.        Specific responsibilities will include:   ·       Teaching up to or the equivalent of two core undergraduate courses directly tied to our character education framework, ensuring pedagogical fidelity and integration with project goals.   ·       Partnering with the PIs to support faculty and student virtue-education training, including workshops, mentoring, classroom sessions, and follow-up engagement.   ·       Supporting the PIs in university-wide advocacy and outreach to other colleges as well as to the broader public.   ·       Assisting the lead PI in designing and leading the biweekly character colloquium (topic selection, readings, integration with curriculum).   ·       Attending weekly project team meetings.   ·       Contributing to assessment planning, execution, and review to enable a rigorous evaluation of student and faculty outcomes.   ·       Coordinating implementation and analysis of the project’s diagnostic framework, ensuring consistent data collection, interpretation, and integration across participating courses.   ·       Collaborating on pedagogical scholarship tied to project activities and outputs, in addition to advancing their own research agenda.   ·       Working with in-house support units, as well as student assistants, to help the PIs develop four Canvas LMS modules for scalable character education use in the University and beyond.      Qualifications   The successful candidate must have:   ·       A PhD (either awarded or submitted and with defense formally scheduled) in a humanistic discipline, with a research specialization related to character education and/or virtue theory.   ·       Demonstrated strong interest and ability in teaching.   ·       Excellent communication, interpersonal, and organizational skills.   ·       Ability to work both independently and as part of a team that must deal professionally with people at all levels.      Preference will be given for those candidates who have applied character education experience and/or a substantive specialization in virtue theory, complemented by demonstrated pedagogical skill.    The successful candidate will participate in implementing and expanding our program across the instructional, developmental, and scholarly domains rather than acting only as a pure research scholar.   Informal queries should be directed to character@fit.edu   Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities. The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following website 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
