    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/Admin-I---Testing-Coordinator---1_R9625-1": {
      "title": "Admin I - Testing Coordinator - 1",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-10\nPart time\n\nThe Student Success and Support Center(SSSC) is now hiring for a part time administrative assistant to assist with test proctoring services in the Student Success and Support Center in support of the Office of Accessibility Resources. The administrative assistant will be responsible for the following: Assist with coordinating exam scheduling and proctoring Assist with file organization and management of incoming and outgoing exams Assist with creating final exam schedules with the SSSC Proctor exams using video monitoring and personal supervision Ensure testing areas are secure, quiet, and in accordance with FL Tech's Academic Honesty Policy Maintain a professional and welcoming testing environment and equipment (ADA materials, ear plugs, calculators, etc) Assist the Office of Accessibility Resources with file organization (summer) Assist SSSC with other duties as needed Education Requirements: High School Diploma or GED Other: Experience working with college students preferred Experience with Test Proctoring Services preferred Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities. The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following website 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/Director-of-Philanthropy--COES_R9636": {
      "title": "Director of Philanthropy, COES",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nFull time\n\nThe Director of Philanthropy is a front line fundraising professional responsible for the management of a portfolio of 100 – 120 prospects comprised of alumni and friends associated with the College of Engineering and Sciences (COES) with capacity and inclination to make major gifts of $25K or more to Florida Tech. Prospect management includes discovery and qualification of new prospects, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship of donors, with a strong focus on cultivating long term relationships and vision of college leaders to secure support for strategics priorities and initiatives. The Director of Philanthropy for COES communicates and travels extensively to develop and strengthen donor relationships. Participates and leads in a broad range of events on and off campus having a strategic impact on the success of individual prospect relationships. The Director works collaboratively with other staff in the Advancement Division, the College of Engineering & Science, as well as university administrators, faculty, and staff. Job Responsibilities Include: Manage a portfolio of COES major gift prospects with a strong focus on cultivating, soliciting, and stewarding long term relationships with donors who have the capacity to make transformative gifts of $25K or more. In conjunction with the Associate Vice President of Campaigns & VP of Philanthropy, establish prospect management goals for visits and solicitations. Develop and keep updated contact reports, opportunities, and solicitation efforts. Ensure the accuracy and timely entry of all information and records into the CRM database (Raiser’s Edge & FPM). Lead monthly meetings with College of Engineering and Sciences Dean & secure prospective donor meetings for university leaderships to deepen relations. Participate in leadership meetings to better understand each unit ' s needs, challenges, and opportunities, as well as help coordinate central advancement efforts in all areas of the department. Requirements Include: Bachelor's in nonprofit management, communication, business or other related fields preferred. 5 - 8 years of experience in nonprofits and/or front-line fundraising of major gifts or planned gifts ($25k+). Preference for higher ed experience. Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities. The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following website 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/APSS---Department-Head_R9656": {
      "title": "APSS - Department Head",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Aerospace, Physics and Space Sciences (APSS) at the Florida Institute of Technology invites applications for a department head with an expected start date of August 2027. We seek a visionary leader with suitable academic and administrative experience to guide a dynamic and multidisciplinary department of approximately 30 faculty members spanning aerospace engineering, astronomy and astrophysics, physics, space physics, astrobiology, and planetary sciences. The ideal candidate will possess the collaborative acumen and leadership background to support interdisciplinary innovation, and champion excellence across both our educational and research missions. We are looking for a leader who can build strong connections across traditional disciplinary boundaries, inspire and mentor early career faculty across all fields, and scale our sponsored research enterprise while maintaining our deep commitment to student success. Key responsibilities and qualifications include demonstrated excellence in holistic leadership, a commitment to fostering a collaborative culture, a distinguished record of scholarly accomplishment, and a dedication to upholding and elevating high standards in graduate and undergraduate education, mentorship, and curriculum development. Candidates are required to hold a Ph.D. in Aerospace Engineering, Physics, Astronomy, Planetary Science, or a closely related field, alongside a distinguished record of academic accomplishment appropriate for appointment as a tenured full professor. To apply, please submit a single PDF document containing a cover letter, CV, a statement of research experience and interests, a statement of teaching experience and philosophy, and a statement of vision describing plans for the growth of the APSS department, and contact information for at least three references. Review of applications will begin November 01, 2026. The position is open until filled with anticipated start date of August 2027. For questions or concerns, please contact our Search Committee at apss-dh-search2026@fit.edu Information about the Department of Aerospace, Physics and Space Sciences and the College of Engineering and Science can be found at https://www.fit.edu/engineering-and-science/ Aerospace research laboratories are focused on autonomy, orbital robotics, fuel tank slosh, and a growing interest in CubeSats. Our high-energy physics group is a member of the CMS collaboration at CERN, and our atmospheric electricity group are world leaders, with a record that includes the discovery of X-ray and gamma-rays from lightning.  Florida Tech is the founding member of the Southeastern Association for Research in Astronomy, established the first undergraduate astrobiology program in the US, and our faculty have a strong history of successful research using a variety of ground-based and space-based facilities.  The APSS department currently serves nearly 700 undergraduate and over 200 graduate students.  We are at the heart of a vibrant research and innovation community on the U.S. Space Coast and nestled in an area of outstanding natural beauty. Melbourne is served by the Melbourne-Orlando International Airport, and is consistently ranked as one of the best places to live in the U.S. Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities. The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following website 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/Event-Operations-Coordinator_R9646": {
      "title": "Event Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\nThe Office of University Events and Protocol oversees all non-academic use of campus venues, summer camps and conferences, intern housing, and internal and external campus events. The office supports Florida Tech's mission of teaching, research, and service through the effective development and management of events that communicate and enhance the university's reputation for excellence, especially those that increase the university's visibility. The university's facilities exist primarily for education, research, and public service. When space is available, Florida Tech is committed to making its facilities available to external groups and organizations whose purposes are consistent with the university's mission. Responsibilities include overseeing centralized scheduling of campus venues, venue management and supervision, student staff supervision, event planning and execution, office support with extensive phone and email work, and data entry. This role requires strong communication skills, attention to detail, and the ability to work effectively in a fast-paced, team-oriented environment. The position requires flexibility in the work schedule due to evening and weekend events. Responsibilities Include: Coordinate logistical support for events with internal service providers and external vendors. Assist with populating contract templates with pertinent information for external clients and third-party vendors. Provide on-site support during event setup, execution, and breakdown to ensure smooth operations. Troubleshoot on-site issues and escalate concerns to Event Managers when necessary. Upon completion of events, confirm accurate billing information, generate invoices and distribute invoices to clients. Reconcile payments in EMS. Evaluate and approve student organization and campus department reservation requests received via the Event Management System (EMS). Input requests and related event data into EMS. Answer department phone calls and emails received to alias email addresses. Answer routine questions and escalate calls as needed, and route to the appropriate staff member. Assist with creating room diagrams in Social Tables for various campus venues. Oversee student setup staff with setups of event venues as necessary. Requirements Include: Bachelor degree in Hospitality Management preferred 1-2 years of related experience required Valid driver’s license Microsoft Office: Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Publisher, Outlook Experience in event scheduling, room reservations, event management, and supervision Excellent organizational and follow-up skills Strong oral, written, and interpersonal skills Ability to establish and maintain good rapport with clients, staff, and collaborate with other departments Knowledge of EMS and Social Tables (or related event management and room diagramming software) is a plus Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities. The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following website 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/HVAC-Mechanical-Supervisor_R9683-1": {
      "title": "HVAC Mechanical Supervisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nThe HVAC Mechanical Supervisor provides leadership and technical oversight for the operation, maintenance, repair, and improvement of Florida Tech's HVAC, mechanical, and Building Automation Systems. The position supervises skilled HVAC and controls personnel while ensuring reliable building conditions, preventive maintenance, energy efficiency, indoor air quality, and responsive customer service. The Supervisor also provides mechanical and controls expertise for construction, renovations, deferred maintenance, and capital renewal projects. The position works closely with Facilities leadership, engineers, contractors, other trades, and campus departments and serves as emergency-required personnel during major equipment failures, hurricanes, and other campus emergencies. This individual will oversee a staff of skilled trades personnel and student workers and will report to the Director of Maintenance. Florida Institute of Technology is a growing, top-ranked university with over 2,000,000 square feet of facilities to maintain. Normal Working Hours are Monday through Friday from 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM, and must be available for after-hour call-backs on a 24/7 basis. This supervisory position reports to the Director of Maintenance. Oversee daily operation and performance of campus HVAC, mechanical, and Building Automation Systems. Order parts or full units and ensure systems provide reliable temperature, humidity, ventilation, and environmental conditions while minimizing disruptions to University operations. Direct, supervise, mentor, and evaluate assigned HVAC and controls personnel. Remove obstacles for staff, establish priorities, assign work, monitor performance, provide technical guidance, and promote employee development, accountability, and safe work practices. Manage preventive and predictive maintenance programs for chillers, air handling units, pumps, controls, and related mechanical equipment. Monitor equipment condition and recurring failures to improve reliability, extend equipment life, and reduce emergency repairs. Oversee BAS programming, scheduling, alarms, trending, sequences of operation, integration, and troubleshooting. Use system data and operational knowledge to improve comfort, equipment performance, reliability, and energy efficiency. Provide HVAC, mechanical, and controls expertise for new construction, renovations, and capital projects. Review designs and equipment selections and coordinate with engineers, contractors, project managers, and Facilities staff through construction, commissioning, turnover, and warranty. Prioritize and coordinate HVAC and BAS corrective maintenance, and ensure work is completed and appropriately documented. Communicate significant problems, outages, repair schedules, and service impacts to Facilities leadership and campus customers. Identify opportunities to reduce energy consumption and operating costs through BAS programming, equipment optimization, scheduling, and mechanical improvements. Support indoor air quality investigations and balance energy conservation with comfort and operational requirements. Assist with operating budgets, deferred maintenance, capital renewal planning, cost estimates, equipment replacement, and scopes of work. Support procurement and contractor selection while promoting cost-conscious use of University resources. Ensure work complies with University policies, safety requirements, applicable codes, and established procedures. Serve as emergency-required personnel and provide operational support during hurricanes, utility interruptions, major equipment failures, and other emergencies. Maintain necessary equipment, system, maintenance, and technical documentation, and coordinate activities with other Facilities teams. Perform additional duties as assigned in support of Facilities Operations and the University. Additional Duties as assigned. Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities. The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following website 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Institute-of-Technology/Security-Officer_R9675": {
      "title": "Security Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nThe Florida Tech Department of Security Officers are responsible for campus surveillance and the patrol of buildings to provide a visible presence that deters crime. Officers respond to all emergencies and routine calls for service in a timely manner and coordinate effectively with police, fire, and emergency medical personnel. Additional responsibilities include providing general safety information through daily interaction with the campus community, monitoring campus activity, special events, and parking areas, assisting members of the community, enforcing traffic and parking regulations, and dispatching calls and answering campus security telephones. The position requires minor physical exertion, including pushing, pulling, carrying, and lifting up to 25 pounds, as well as the ability to stand and/or walk for extended periods without rest or assistance. Department of Security Officers operate within a 24-hour, 7-days-a-week environment. Due to the nature of the position, officers are expected to work an 80-hour pay cycle with a fluctuating schedule, including nights, weekends, and holidays. Role and Expectations: Security Officers are required to successfully complete all basic training requirements and actively participate in the department’s Field Training Program. Officers must maintain proficiency in weapons familiarization in accordance with departmental standards. This position is considered entry-level and developmental in nature; therefore, assignments will initially be limited in scope, with restricted independent responsibilities until required competencies and performance standards are demonstrated. Responsibilities Include: Serve as a visible and proactive university representative 24/7, provide a secure environment for the university students, affiliates, and visitors.  Conduct patrols via foot, bicycle, golf cart, and vehicle, provide services, access functions, and escorts as requested.  Provide safety awareness through daily contact with the university community and visitors by providing general safety information, directions, and parking information.  Identify actual or potential safety hazards or conditions and coordinate a resolution with the appropriate department or area.  Provide assistance to university personnel in matters involving potential violations of the University policy wherein conduct or behavioral expectations are disruptive to the mission of the university. Respond to all emergencies, coordinate law enforcement, medical, and /or fire assistance, provide a proactive role after the emergency has concluded, and make appropriate notifications on campus as directed. Prepare incident reports and related written communication, maintain activity logs, and utilize the Computer Aided Dispatch (CAD) system as appropriate, answer telephone calls, and dispatch calls via two-way radio. Work special events to provide traffic, parking, and crowd control as needed. Assist the parking program by monitoring parking areas, issuing citations, and maintaining access to fire lanes, handicap spaces, and roadways owned and/or controlled by the University. Performs initial Title IX investigations, ensuring all appropriate information and counseling are provided to the complainant and then forwarded to the appropriate staff.  Any other related duties as assigned by the Department of Security administration or shift supervisors are included within the Security Officer’s responsibilities.  May be required to work during emergency situations, which may require the officer to remain at the university until the emergency situation has terminated. Work designated alternate hours and perform other responsibilities as needed. Requirements Include: High School Diploma Required. Additional certifications, licensing, degrees, and/or training in law enforcement, security, or related fields are preferred. Must have the ability to obtain a State of Florida, Class D: Security Officer License and a State of Florida, Class G: Armed Security Officer License within 30 days of the hire date. Must have a valid Florida driver's license and maintain a good driving record as determined by Florida Tech guidelines. Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities. The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following website 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/Electrician_R9682": {
      "title": "Electrician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nFlorida Institute of Technology is a growing, top-ranked university with more than 2 million square feet of facilities supporting a dynamic campus community. The Electrician performs skilled electrical installation, maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair across the university’s facilities and infrastructure. This position helps ensure electrical systems and equipment operate safely, reliably, and in compliance with applicable codes and standards. Key responsibilities include preventive and corrective maintenance, responding to service requests and emergency situations, and supporting electrical projects, renovations, and facility improvements. The successful candidate will demonstrate strong technical knowledge, sound judgment, attention to detail, and a commitment to safety and quality. Schedule: Monday–Friday, 8:00 a.m.–4:30 p.m., with occasional overtime as needed. Maintain lighting and security lighting on campus. Repair all electrical equipment on the main campus and at remote sites. Complete scheduled preventative maintenance. Complete daily service request as assigned. Installation of new electrical equipment, ballasts, fixtures, and lamps. Carry a rotating cellphone for after-hours emergency calls – may be required to work evenings and weekends in an emergency. Emergency preparedness and support as directed. Additional duties as assigned. Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities. The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following website 2025 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
