    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/Staff-Clinician---Staff-Psychologist_R9702": {
      "title": "Staff Clinician / Staff Psychologist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nThe Student Counseling Center is seeking to hire a licensed mental health clinician with a strong interest and experience in collegiate mental health. The center welcomes applications from skilled masters-level and doctoral-level generalists, who thrive in an environment that values collaboration among team members and helps cultivate a healthy campus environment. This position will have opportunities to enhance the resources of our team and shape how the center strategically interacts with the campus community. In this position, the primary responsibility is to provide direct clinical services to Florida Tech students, including individual, couples, and group counseling, as well as crisis intervention and management. Experience working with a wide range of mental health concerns and levels of severity is necessary to serve the variety of clinical needs. In addition to clinical activities, there is opportunity to meaningfully add to the center’s training and outreach/consultation initiatives. Responsibilities Include: Clinical Services Offer culturally competent and evidence-based assessment and psychotherapy services to students presenting with a broad range of concerns and diagnoses, and provide consultation and referral services, when appropriate, to coordinate care. Conduct crisis intervention and management services and be able to initiate involuntary examination procedures in accordance with the Florida Mental Health Act, when appropriate. Supervision and Training Provide education, training, and/or supervision to students enrolled in the APA-accredited doctoral program for clinical psychology. Outreach and Consultation Utilize expert knowledge to consult with the campus community regarding topics related to student mental health, safety, and well-being. Participate in campus crisis response activities following disruptive events. Other Duties  Engage in professional development and service activities that facilitate increased knowledge and experience in collegiate mental health. Coordinate specific center programming areas consistent with interests and expertise. Minimum Qualifications Master’s or Doctoral degree in a mental health field from an accredited college or university. Licensed as a Clinical Social Worker, Mental Health Counselor, or Psychologist by the State of Florida. Experience utilizing a variety of culturally competent and evidence-based assessment and psychotherapy modalities, as well as in providing crisis intervention, outreach/consultation, and referral services. Ability to manage the professional demands of a fast-paced and challenging workload, utilizing strong time management, organizational, and verbal and written communication skills.  Ability to think critically and make clear, ethical, and timely decisions to manage complex cases and to provide supervision, training, and/or consultation, as needed. Knowledge of professional ethical principles/guidelines, relevant state laws, and the ethical and legal issues regarding mental health practice, as well as the ability to interpret/apply laws, policies, and procedures consistently. Ability to work both as a member of a team and independently with minimal supervision. Preferred Qualifications Previous experience working in a university counseling center and in providing crisis intervention and short-term psychotherapy services to a diverse clientele with a full range of mental health concerns. Interest and experience in providing supervision and training activities with students at the doctoral level. Administrative and computer application skills to effectively implement and maintain a case management and tracking system. Required Application Materials To ensure full consideration, an application, letter of interest, vitae/resume, and at least letter of recommendation should be included in the application packet. Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus.   The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities.   The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following web site 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/Dean--College-of-Business_R9716": {
      "title": "Dean, College of Business",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nFlorida Tech seeks a visionary, entrepreneurial, and collaborative Dean to lead the success, growth, and future of the College of Business. Reporting directly to the Provost and Senior Vice President for Academic Affairs, the Dean will provide strategic leadership across academic programs, research and scholarship, faculty development, student success initiatives, fundraising, corporate partnerships, and global engagement. The successful candidate will serve as the chief academic and administrative officer of the College, leading a multidisciplinary faculty and professional staff while fostering innovation, promoting interdisciplinary collaboration, strengthening business and industry partnerships, and ensuring the highest standards of academic quality and ethical practice. Qualifications   The successful candidate must possess:   An earned doctorate or equivalent terminal degree from an accredited institution in business or a closely related discipline. Scholarly, professional, or practice-based achievements warranting appointment as a tenured Full Professor within the College of Business. A distinguished record of teaching, academic, professional, and administrative accomplishment. Demonstrated experience in academic leadership, including program-level, departmental, collegiate, or university-level strategic planning and decision-making. In addition, the successful candidate will demonstrate the following preferred qualifications: A minimum of five years of progressive academic leadership experience, with prior service as a dean, associate dean, department chair, center director, or comparable leadership role strongly preferred. Strong communication, interpersonal, and consensus-building skills. Proven success in fundraising, corporate partnership development, and alumni engagement. Experience collaborating with industry, professional associations, government agencies, and international business organizations. Demonstrated understanding of business school accreditation processes (e.g., IACBE, AACSB, ACBSP or equivalent) and continuous improvement frameworks. Record of successful faculty recruitment, retention, and mentoring. Familiarity with emerging areas such as data analytics, fintech, entrepreneurship, global business, supply chain management, sustainability, and digital transformation. Experience with executive education, online and hybrid program delivery, and workforce-aligned credentialing.   Responsibilities   Academic Leadership   Provide strategic vision and leadership for the College of Business. Oversee curriculum development, assessment, and continuous improvement across undergraduate, graduate, and professional programs. Ensure academic quality, innovation, and alignment with workforce and industry needs. Foster interdisciplinary collaboration within the college and across Florida Tech. Ensure programs maintain appropriate academic accreditations and certifications. Develop and support collaborative degree programs across colleges.   Faculty and Staff Management Lead recruitment, retention, evaluation, and professional development of faculty and staff. Conduct annual performance reviews and support faculty promotion and tenure processes. Facilitate mentoring and leadership development for junior and mid-career faculty. Promote a collegial, inclusive, and high-performing academic culture. Research, Innovation, and Professional Practice Champion impactful scholarship, applied research, and industry-engaged practice aligned with business disciplines. Support faculty in securing external funding, corporate sponsorships, and philanthropic support. Promote student engagement in internships, consulting projects, case competitions, and experiential learning. Encourage innovation, entrepreneurship, and technology-enabled business education.   Student Success and Career Outcomes Advance student recruitment, retention, and graduation initiatives. Strengthen career readiness, placement, and employer engagement. Expand internship pipelines, co-op opportunities, and experiential learning programs. Support graduate and executive education offerings that align with regional and global workforce needs.   Financial and Administrative Leadership Manage college budget, resources, and enrollment planning effectively. Partner with university leadership on financial strategy, program growth, and operational efficiency. Collaborate with University Advancement to develop and implement fundraising strategies. Maintain a portfolio of corporate partners and donors across business sectors.   External Relations and Engagement Serve as the chief representative of the College of Business to external constituencies. Build and sustain strong relationships with corporations, entrepreneurs, alumni, advisory boards, and professional organizations. Promote the College’s visibility, reputation, and thought leadership regionally, nationally, and globally. Expand executive education, professional development, and community engagement initiatives. Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus.   The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities.   The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following web site 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/Research-Development-Specialist_R9713": {
      "title": "Research Development Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nThe Research Development Specialist will support faculty in identifying and pursuing external funding opportunities and developing competitive grant proposals. This position provides strategic funding guidance, proposal development support, editorial review, and project coordination for individual and multi-investigator submissions. The specialist serves as a key partner to faculty throughout the proposal lifecycle, helping investigators identify funding opportunities, strengthen proposal narratives, coordinate interdisciplinary teams, and navigate complex proposal development processes. Key Responsibilities : Funding Opportunity Identification Conduct proactive searches for federal, state, foundation, and industry funding opportunities Stay up-to-date on the latest trends in funding opportunities and shifts in federal and non-federal agency priorities Match funding opportunities to faculty expertise and strategic institutional priorities Provide targeted funding recommendations Facilitate interactions with Program Officers Proposal Development Consult with faculty on proposal strategy and competitiveness Review proposal drafts and provide substantive feedback Assist with development of project narratives, broader impacts, significance statements, and commercialization sections Facilitate proposal planning meetings and timelines Support resubmissions based on reviewer feedback Participate in review of limited submission proposals Multi-PI and Large Proposal Coordination Coordinate multidisciplinary proposal teams Organize planning meetings and proposal development schedules Track contributions, deadlines, and required documents Facilitate communication among investigators and administrative offices Support center-scale, consortium, and institutional proposals Faculty Development Provide one-on-one proposal coaching Conduct workshops on grantsmanship Develop proposal templates, guides, and best practices Assist new faculty with establishing funding strategies   Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree (Master's degree or higher in a relevant field preferred) Experience in research development, grant writing, sponsored programs, research administration, or related field Demonstrated experience identifying funding opportunities Strong writing, editing, and proposal review skills Exceptional project management and organizational skills Ability to work effectively with faculty from diverse disciplines Preferred Qualifications Minimum three years experience supporting proposals to NSF, NIH, DoD, DOE, NASA, or major foundations Familiarity with federal sponsor requirements Experience coordinating interdisciplinary and multi-investigator proposals Certification from NORDP, NCURA, SRAI, or related organizations Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus.   The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities.   The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following web site 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/OEMS-Visiting-Professor_R9712": {
      "title": "OEMS Visiting Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Ocean Engineering and Marine Sciences (OEMS) in Florida Tech’s College of Engineering and Science invites applications for the Doherty Visiting Professorship in the Spring Semester 2027. The endowment to OEMS from the Henry L. and Grace Doherty Charitable Foundation, Inc., provides funding for the appointment.   Our focus is to integrate science, engineering, and management in the marine environment. OEMS offers undergraduate and graduate degrees in ocean engineering, oceanography, meteorology, environmental science and sustainability, environmental resource management, marine biology and ecology, and conservation biology.   We seek an established professional from academia, government, or industry with an advanced degree in ocean engineering or ocean science and a clear vision for her/his time at the University. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute through a combination of teaching in the Ocean Engineering program, research, and student engagement, with specific responsibilities negotiated at the time of appointment.   Applicants should upload a statement of interest not exceeding two pages in length, a two-page curriculum vitae, and a cover letter including contact information for three references to the FIT job portal . The documents should also be sent to Dr. Steven M. Lazarus, Professor, Department of Ocean Engineering and Marine Sciences, Florida Institute of Technology, at slazarus@fit.edu . Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus.   The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities.   The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following web site 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of