    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/OEMS-Assistant-Professor-in-Ocean-Engineering--tenure-track-_R9735": {
      "title": "OEMS Assistant Professor in Ocean Engineering (tenure-track)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nSuccessful candidates are expected to: -Teach courses within our undergraduate and graduate programs. -Advise students on academic matters and career planning. -Serve on College and University committees as assigned. -Obtain external funding for, and/or conduct research in contemporary issues. -Supervise graduate students conducting research. Minimum Qualifications: -Ph.D. degree in a related field. -University teaching experience. Desired Qualifications: -History of research and publication. -Experience with learning management systems and virtual communication technology. -Current professional qualifications, as appropriate. -Experience in teaching statistics at the graduate level. The Department of Ocean Engineering and Marine Sciences at the Florida Institute of Technology invites applications for a 9-month, tenure-track faculty position in Ocean Engineering, with expertise in one or more of these areas: (1) marine materials engineering, (2) offshore renewable energy, and/or (3) Naval Architecture. We are particularly interested in candidates who would complement or augment our existing strengths in naval architecture, underwater vehicles, and coastal restoration. Examples include but are not limited to (1) the use of composites and metals in the marine environment, (2) the design and deployment of ocean-energy systems, and (3) the fundamentals of naval architecture and design of related systems. The successful candidate will be expected to teach both undergraduate and graduate courses; conduct and maintain an active, extramurally funded research program; publish scholarly work; mentor undergraduate and graduate students; and engage in service activities. Applicants must have earned a doctorate in a relevant field, have a strong commitment to undergraduate teaching, and have an interest in interdisciplinary, collaborative research. Industrial experience will be considered an asset. Salary will be commensurate with experience.   Please upload a single PDF document that contains the following items in the order listed: cover letter, curriculum vitae, contact information for three (3) professional references, a statement of research interests, and a statement of teaching interests and teaching philosophy. Questions should be addressed to Dr. Stephen Wood, Department of Ocean Engineering and Marine Sciences, Florida Tech, Melbourne, FL, 32901 ( swood@fit.edu ). Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus.   The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities.   The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following web site 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/Director-of-Student-Academic-Advising_R9730": {
      "title": "Director of Student Academic Advising",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nServes as the Director of Student Advising. Provides leadership and has supervisory responsibility for the administration and the overall operation of the academic advising program. Oversees the operations, staff, and budget for the Office of Student Advising, is responsible for the continued maintenance and evaluation of advising related activities and materials. Supervises all full-time advising staff members and student assistants working for the Office of Student Advising during academic year. Coordinates advising efforts with stakeholders within each college and across campus (including but not limited to Academic Support Services, Career Services, Accessibility Services, Registrar's Office, and Academic Affairs) to provide the appropriate support for students. The Director of Academic Advising provides leadership, strategic direction, and operational oversight for Florida Tech's centralized undergraduate academic advising program. Reporting to the Senior Associate Provost for Academic Affairs, the Director is responsible for developing and administering advising services that promote student success, retention, academic progress, and timely degree completion.   The Director supervises professional academic advisors, establishes advising policies and procedures, oversees assessment and continuous improvement efforts, and collaborates with academic and administrative units to enhance the student experience. The Director serves as a key member of institutional staff in supporting undergraduate student success initiatives and ensuring consistent, high-quality advising services across the university. Responsibilities Provide strategic leadership and administrative oversight for Florida Tech's undergraduate academic advising program. Develop and implement advising strategies, policies, procedures, operational priorities, and performance goals that support student success, retention, progression, and degree completion. Direct advising operations, including staffing, budget administration, resource allocation, service delivery, and process improvement. Recruit, supervise, develop, and evaluate professional advising staff, establishing performance expectations and fostering a culture of accountability, collaboration, and continuous improvement. Build and maintain collaborative partnerships with academic colleges, faculty, Enrollment Management, Student Affairs, Institutional Research, and other campus units to support student success initiatives. Support the effective use of advising technologies, student information systems, and related processes to enhance advising operations and student engagement. Ensure compliance with university policies, academic regulations, FERPA, and applicable federal and state requirements. Represent academic advising on university committees and institutional initiatives related to student success. Participate in professional development activities and maintain knowledge of emerging trends and best practices in academic advising and student success. Minimum Qualifications Master's degree from an accredited institution in Higher Education Administration, Student Affairs, Counseling, Education, or a related field. Five (5) years of progressively responsible experience in academic advising, student success, enrollment management, or a related area within higher education. Two (2) years of supervisory experience. Demonstrated experience leading programs, initiatives, or services designed to improve student success. Experience utilizing data and assessment results to support decision-making. Strong interpersonal, organizational, analytical, and communication skills. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with students, faculty, staff, and administrators. Preferred Qualifications Seven (7) or more years of progressively responsible experience in academic advising or student success leadership. Experience managing a centralized academic advising operation. Experience supervising professional academic advisors. Experience with Banner, Degree Works, Navigate, Argos, or other student information and advising systems commonly used in higher education. Knowledge of NACADA competencies and higher education best practices in academic advising. Experience developing retention, progression, and graduation initiatives. Proficiency with student information systems, advising technologies, and Microsoft Office applications. Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus.   The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities.   The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following web site 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
    },
    "https://floridatech.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/FloridaTechCareers/job/Florida-Tech-Main-Campus-Melbourne/Advancement-Operations-Coordinator_R9741": {
      "title": "Advancement Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nThe Advancement Operations Coordinator supports fundraising and donor engagement efforts through gift processing, database management, donor stewardship, event coordination, and administrative support. This role ensures accurate record keeping, exceptional donor service, and efficient advancement operations. Responsibilities Include : Maintain and execute standard operating procedures (SOPs) to record charitable contributions and grants. Ensure data accuracy, confidentiality, and compliance with all applicable local, state, and federal laws and regulations. Maintain constituent relationship management (CRM) software by creating new records, running audits, removing duplicates, and managing data integrity. Assist with administrative projects, data requests, and special initiatives. Help coordinate advancement events, and provide cross-departmental administrative help. Monitor pledge payments and generate payment reminders. Prepare reports on fundraising appeal activity, donor annual giving and summary reports. Support gift reconciliation with finance and accounting systems. Ensure donors receive timely and accurate receipts for charitable contributions. Respond promptly and professionally to donor inquiries received by phone, email, or mail. Other tasks as assigned by Advancement leadership in support of Advancement and/or Florida Tech's mission. Requirements Include : Minimum 0–2 years of relevant work experience preferred. Equal Opportunity Florida Institute of Technology does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national or ethnic origin, religion, disability, age, genetic information, marital status, citizenship status, veteran status, and other legally protected characteristics in its activities and programs. In accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Florida Tech does not discriminate on the basis of sex.  Inquiries concerning the application of the Non-Discrimination and Anti-Harassment policy may be directed to the Equal Opportunity Director and Investigator at 150 West University Blvd, Melbourne, FL 32901, or email equalopp@fit.edu , or +1 321-674-7153; or to the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights. Americans with Disabilities Act Please notify us at least five days in advance if a reasonable accommodation for a disability is needed by calling the Office of Human Resources at 321-674-8100. Annual Security & Fire Safety Report The federal Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act) requires colleges and universities, both public and private, participating in federal student aid programs to disclose campus safety information, and imposes certain basic requirements for handling incidents of sexual violence and emergency situations. Florida Tech’s 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Florida Tech; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus.   The numbers provided include crime statistics that were reported to local law enforcement agencies, campus security and other Campus Security Authorities.   The report also includes institutional policy statements regarding campus safety and security measures; descriptions of prevention and awareness programs; related university procedures and important guidance; and other essential safety information. You can obtain a printed copy of this report at the Department of Security Welcome Center located at 3126 Panther Place, during normal business hours, or by accessing the following web site 2026 Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Official Transcripts Official transcripts of all collegiate work must be sent directly from the attended institution to the Office of Human Resources prior to the first day of employment. All international degrees must have a course-by-course official evaluation and translation sent to the Human Resources Office directly from an evaluation company affiliated with the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services, Inc. (NACES). We are an E-verify employer"
